Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
this morning we are focusing on  something millions of americans are  dealing with every single day but very  few talk about it more than six million  women in this country have trouble  getting or staying pregnant that's  according to the cdc now that's about 10  percent of those who were of  childbearing age tv personality and  founder of the lv guide that's liliana  vasquez when she realized it was going  to be difficult for her to get pregnant  she kept it to herself for national  infertility awareness week we asked  liliana to share her own path to  motherhood with cbs mornings  in the fall of 2015 i was asked to sit  on the panel of a new network show  for a girl from fort worth texas who  spoke english as her second language let  me tell you it was a dream come true one  morning right before we went live my  cell phone rang  my stomach dropped as soon as i saw the  number on my screen  it was my ob gyn calling with blood work  results women are born with around 2  million eggs which is all the eggs  they'll ever have  at the onset of menopause most women  only have around a thousand left  my doctor called to share that at just  35 years young my hormone levels  indicated that i could be dangerously  close to that number  she followed up with the one question  that changed the course of the next six  years  do you want to be a mother  i still get emotional saying that  question out loud  because up until that moment i hadn't  really thought about it i was the career  girl  i was the woman who prioritized my job  above almost all else  now here i was broken on a bathroom  floor  crying over something i never realized i  wanted so badly  when i finally composed myself  i answered her question with a  resounding yes  but my journey to motherhood would not  come easily  infertility had taken agency over my  body i was ashamed  embarrassed and i didn't want anyone to  know  so i made a decision to keep my  diagnosis as private as possible  over the next few years i saw every  specialist i could find hundreds of  needles filled containers in my kitchen  cabinets i cannot wait until all of this  is finally over each one was a testament  to the battle i was fighting tiny swords  i wielded each day in my war on  infertility  months turned to years and before i knew  it  we had lost seven embryos  back at it again  i started to lose track of all of the  appointments the ultrasounds the blood  draws  i started to lose track of myself  when you're battling infertility a bad  day feels unbearable  a good day still feels hopeless  each failed cycle made me question  myself was i woman enough good enough or  even committed enough to get pregnant  could we be happy if life for just the  two of us  was i enough  for me  faith came in the form of one word  i remember walking off offset defeated  after yet another negative pregnancy  test when a co-worker came over and  whispered surrender  her simple instruction started a seismic  shift in the acceptance of my journey i  learned how to release the idea of  pregnancy and instead opened my heart to  parenthood  i stopped obsessing over the statistics  that would never be in my favor  my worth wouldn't be tied to a follicle  count or an fsh level anymore because no  matter what the number said  i had always been worthy  of becoming a mother on election day  2020  we transferred one of our last embryos  as they wheeled me into the room i did  my best to let go  i closed my eyes and surrendered to our  greatest wish of all  just to be parents  santi lived only in our hearts for six  years  but then  on july 19 2021 our brave beautiful boy  came into this world and healed my  heart every night i feel his tiny body  relax and somehow grow so heavy in my  arms i can see the outline of his  beautiful lashes that perfect nose  and his little lips pursed together  somehow telling me without words mama  thank you  i'm safe  and happy  and so loved  ivf showed me how to grieve and grow and  that faith arrives when you face your  biggest fears  i learned there's freedom and  forgiveness and above all  boundless strength and vulnerability  liliana is here lily i say mama thank  you too did you ever think you could  feel such love you would kill for this  little creature i know it i absolutely  would i think i said it too best i love  my husband with all my heart i'd  probably kill him for this baby too i  mean i don't know that i've ever felt  this and i didn't know that that was a  feeling that was missing for me for so  long what struck me about your piece  number one it's beautifully done and  very very powerful when you said you go  you were ashamed and embarrassed and you  didn't want to say thank you for getting  over that and sharing your story with  all of us but what were you ashamed and  embarrassed about this was something  that wasn't beyond your control we have  to dig into why people women feel this  way you know for me i felt broken and i  felt like the one thing i should be able  to do is get pregnant and have a baby  it's all i had thought was easy right  and then all of a sudden i couldn't do  it no matter how tri how hard i tried i  couldn't control that and i feel like so  many women who are battling ivf feel so  much shame in that why can't my body  just do this one thing um and i didn't  want to share that because i didn't want  to be pitied either you know i think  that's really important for me i wanted  to  be strong and confident and i didn't  want people to look at me and be like oh  she can't get pregnant yeah  but you weren't even sure you wanted a  baby in the beginning it's only when the  doctor asked that question absolutely  true absolutely you know i was never  that person who picked out names in  advance and it wasn't until that very  moment i think and it happens a lot in  life when people  take something away from you or the  opportunity to have something is taken  away from you it becomes crystal clear  all i could see was myself as a mother  at that point yeah it's a very  clarifying moment you're only 35 years  old at the time you thought you had  years to go i had no i thought i could  have a baby whenever i wanted it it was  on my schedule according to my plan it  wasn't you're a career woman you think  yes and that was the most important  thing for you that was what my focus was  on right it was on my relationship my  friends it was on travel it was  adventure and my focus wasn't on being a  mom or being a parent that could come in  time but it wasn't what i really wanted  in that moment until my doctor asked  that question and that is really what  changed the course of the next six years  of my life for me and i had never been  more grounded and more clear in the fact  that i wanted to be a mother and i  wanted to be a parent i was telling you  while the piece was running that i love  that you used the technical terms for  the follicle counts and all this because  the people who are going through this  you know the average person may not know  what it is but the millions of people  going through this they know what that  is oh you know you hear fsh and you go  oh god not again not again what is it  how low is it or follicle counts right  those numbers dominated my life for so  long until i was finally able to let  that go so now that you're on the other  side of it what what is your message to  those people those millions of people  who are in it right now you know i think  the biggest shift for me was shifting  from fear to acceptance right and and  what does that mean that means that when  you're going into these appointments you  know fear says oh i only have six  follicles or fear says we only have two  embryos but what acceptance is is it  only takes one it only takes one egg and  it only took one for me to have santiago  and that shift is what gives people hope  and i really hope that people see this  and know that there's still hope i think  it's so important that you tell your  story because i remember you know very  ignorantly on my part years ago a couple  had been married a couple years and i  said are you guys planning to have  children they said well yeah and i said  well what are you waiting for it's easy  i mean that's so stupid when you don't  know and she goes actually gail it's not  and the way she looked at me i felt such  shame that i because i'm trying to be  hahaha funny i felt such shame that i  had done that to her that's why it's so  important that you educate us about  what we may or may not know because when  you said when battling a bad day feels  unbearable a good day feels still feels  hopeless absolutely because there's bad  news at every turn and there's so much  loss in ivf it's not just you know  getting pregnant and having a  miscarriage when you're going through  ivf you're losing embryos you're  canceling transfers you know there is so  much trauma in that and everything  becomes a trigger and so when somebody  says something like that even though you  know you're joking and trying to be  light about it but it's not funny it's  not funny and it's so deeply painful and  i think the more we talk about it the  more we normalize ivf and infertility  people will stop in second guess asking  that question because you never know  what somebody is fighting for privately  and the best thing about santi is what  his laugh you heard it right you heard  it right there that baby but by the way  you got to work for those laughs he's  not that baby that's serving them up at  every corner so every day i'm out there  like doing the craziest dances and  making the faces but that laugh lights  up my heart and it'll light up our lives  for for forever perfect little nose as  well oh happy for you liliana thank you  so much happy ending to the story thank  you for having me here to share more on  we needed you today oh thank you juliana  vasquez we appreciate it

